Implement user story AMSD-2041: [GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Story Description
[GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Acceptance Criteria
- 


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/services/contentstack.ts** (`getSingletonEntry`): This is where Contentstack queries are constructed via ContentstackFactory.createContentTypeQuery. The Stack is initialized with live_preview config (lines 62-66), but the query functions (getSingletonEntry, findSingletonEntry, getEntry) never pass the live_preview hash from URL params to the queries — so even with live_preview enabled on the Stack, queries always return published content, never draft/preview content. This is the CAUSE: the data computation layer, not the presentation layer.
  - **Minimal fix:** Add a live_preview hash parameter (sourced from URL query params via getStaticProps/getServerSideProps context) to the IContentstackGetSingletonEntry and IContentstackGetEntry interfaces, then pass it as .includeFallback() or as the live_preview query param on the Contentstack query chain before .find()/.findOne(). The same change applies to findSingletonEntry (line ~295-305) and getEntry (line ~171). Additionally, getStaticProps in src/pages/[[...slug]].tsx must read context.preview or the live_preview URL param and forward it through to these service calls, and the app needs a pages/api/preview.ts route to enable Next.js draft mode so that getStaticProps re-runs on CMS edits.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): The provider currently only passes static defaultContent via useMemo with no subscription mechanism. For live preview to update the UI in real-time as the editor types, the provider must subscribe to Contentstack live preview events (via @contentstack/live-preview-utils onEntryChange) and re-fetch/update the content object, which then propagates through useContent to all 236+ consuming components.
  - **Minimal fix:** Add a useEffect inside ContentstackProvider that calls contentstack.LivePreview.init() (from @contentstack/live-preview-utils, already a peer of the contentstack SDK) and subscribes via onEntryChange to re-fetch content from the Stack with the live_preview hash, updating the context state so all useContent consumers re-render with draft content. The useMemo dependency on defaultContent alone is insufficient — it must also depend on a livePreviewContent state updated by the subscription callback.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When a content editor opens a page in live preview mode with unpublished draft content available in the CMS, the rendered page displays the draft content values, and those displayed values are present and correct for the draft entry.
- When a visitor opens a page outside of any live preview session, the rendered page displays the published content, and the displayed headings, body text, and images are present and correct for the published entry.
- When a page is opened without an active or valid live preview session, the rendered page displays the published content and no draft or unpublished content is visible anywhere on the page.
- When a live preview content request fails or the preview content cannot be retrieved, the rendered page still displays the published content, and the displayed content is present and correct with no blank, broken, or error state shown to the user.
- When a content editor modifies content in the CMS while viewing the page in live preview mode, the updated content becomes visible on the rendered page as the edits are made.
- The live preview behavior is observable on each of the GO, UP, and MX market sites: an editor previewing draft content on any of these sites sees that site's draft content displayed correctly on the rendered page.

